; One rule, application: a proof of (-> A B) is a function from proofs of A to
; proofs of B, and a logic's rules are data, proofs whose types are arrows.
; The negative: in a row, $p, $q and $r are unknowns of the state, so the
; Deduction held as a row is one instance in each outcome (A to C, B to D, or
; C onwards), never the schema; no outcome composes twice.  Under step every
; the three applications of Deduction tell its unknowns three values at once,
; and there is no step at all.

(: a A)
(: ab (-> A B))
(: bc (-> B C))
(: cd (-> C D))
(: Deduction (-> (-> $p $q) (-> (-> $q $r) (-> $p $r))))

(rule apply
  (read (: $f (-> $a $b)) (: $x $a))
  (make (: ($f $x) $b)))

(step one)
(store set)
